<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.5.5: Choosing Your Own Method to Factor Trinomials</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01654</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.5.5: Choosing Your Own Method to Factor Trinomials</md:title>
    <md:uuid>079adbf0-b330-4817-8248-2226cfd4fdd9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Work with a partner. For each trinomial, explain which method you would use to factor. When you have discussed your reasoning, factor the trinomial and compare solutions.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(
    x^2 − 14x − 32\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>trial and error; for example, \((x − 16)(x + 2)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(6r^2 + 30r + 36\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> the “\(ac\)” method; for example, \(6(r + 3)(r + 2)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((x + 3)^2 − 9(x + 3) − 36\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> substitution; for example, \((x − 9)(x + 6)\)</para></section>
</content>
</document>
